 are following.</p></section><section><h2>2. Learn the tiles</h2><p>Identify the three numbered suits, winds, dragons, flowers and jokers. American sets generally contain 152 tiles; check the exact contents of yours.</p></section><section><h2>3. Build the walls and deal</h2><p>Shuffle tiles face down, construct the walls and deal according to your group's rules. One player begins as East.</p></section><section><h2>4. Play the Charleston</h2><p>American Mah Jongg commonly begins with a structured tile-passing sequence called the Charleston. Follow the correct directions and optional stages for your ruleset.</p></section><section><h2>5. Draw, discard and call</h2><p>On your turn, draw a tile and discard one while working toward a legal pattern on the annual card. Learn the restrictions on calling discards and using jokers.</p></section><section><h2>6. Declare Mahjong</h2><p>Win only when your tiles satisfy an eligible hand on the current card and any applicable concealed or exposed requirements. A group member or teacher can help verify the result.</p></section><aside><strong>A note on official rules</strong><p>This is an independent educational overview, not a substitute for the current official National Mah Jongg League card or rules. We do not reproduce copyrighted annual card patterns.</p></aside><h2>Keep exploring</h2><div class="related"><a href="/">a →</a><a href="S">e →</a><a href="/">a →</a><a href="U">n →</a><a href="/#tools">Free tools →</a></div></main><footer>© {new Date().getFullYear()} My Mahjong Era · Independent American Mahjong learning and lifestyle</footer></body></html>